# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever is checking out.
#
# The whole test suite reads its own source and matches it with regexes —
# `assert.match(mainSource, /…\n\s+…/)` and a couple of hundred more like it,
# across twenty-odd files. Those regexes are written with `\n` because the
# repository is written with `\n`. On a checkout that converts to CRLF they
# cannot match ANYTHING, and the failure does not look like a line-ending
# problem: it looks like six unrelated tests suddenly asserting that the
# mansion has no cellar boundary, the Palace has no acoustics, and the
# initiation's pistol changes hands twice.
#
# That is not hypothetical. It is what the recording pass hit on Windows,
# and it cost somebody an afternoon proving the failures were not theirs
# (they stashed every change and re-ran against pristine main to be sure).
#
# So: LF in the working tree, on every platform, for everything git thinks
# is text. `tests/source-line-endings.test.mjs` keeps it that way.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. Most are already detected, but an mp3 or a
# png that got misdetected as text once would be corrupted by normalisation,
# and a corrupted take is a lot harder to notice than a failing regex.
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
